// A program hands the host a checkpoint whose certificate evidence is false,
// and `restore` refuses it. `gandr run false-checkpoint.gandr` writes the
// refusal as one line on standard error. The run stops at `restore`, and it
// prints nothing.
//
// The text is the checkpoint of a machine on `sqrt 2 < 3/2` after two quanta,
// one for each pole, except for one byte. The first step of each pole
// witnesses the cut of `sqrt 2`: `1` lies in its lower cut, and `2` lies in
// its upper cut. The text moves the lower point of the pole that holds to
// `3`, which does not lie in the lower cut. The bytes stay canonical, and the
// checker finds the false point only when it replays the steps.
//
// `restore` validates the plan again and replays the steps again. It refuses
// the checkpoint at step 0 by the check `NotLower`.
//
// - see: `corpus/model/reals/resume.gandr` — a checkpoint that resumes
// - see: `crates/theory-exact-reals/src/dovetail.rs` — the checkpoint of the
//   scheduler

// The console's host interface.
import std.io;
// The exact reals' host interface.
import std.reals;

/// The text of the checkpoint with its lower point at `3`. It holds the
/// length of the plan and the plan in 180 bytes, then the checkpoint of the
/// scheduler in 147 bytes.
pub def falseCheckpoint : String = "";

/// The entry: restore the machine of the text and step it once. The host
/// declines `restore`, so the run stops there and prints nothing.
def main() -> [std.reals.Eff, std.io.Console.Eff] Integer =
  let machine = std.reals.restore falseCheckpoint
  let *{verdict, same} = std.reals.step machine
  std.io.Console.print verdict
  0
